Loaded playbook: adversarial_review
Description: Adversarial verification of a draft report. Treats every finding as probably wrong until independently reproduced, requires a control test for each, checks for duplicates and for anything dropped from the raw notes, and returns CONFIRMED / REFUTED / UNPROVEN with evidence. 
Mode: agent

╭───────────────────────────────────────────── Starting ─────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                                │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                                      │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                                    │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                                        │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                                     │
│ Max loops: 75                                                                                      │
│ Task: Adversarial verification of a draft report. Treats every finding as probably wrong until     │
│ independently reproduced, requires a control test for each, checks for duplicates and for anything │
│ dropped from the raw notes, and returns CONFIRMED / REFUTED / UNPROVEN with evidence.              │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Take the draft apart                                                                        │
│ Objective: Turn prose into a checkable list of claims.                                             │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Read the draft report and list every distinct claim: what is asserted, against which          │
│ endpoint, with what evidence, and at what severity and proof level.                                │
│   2. Separately read the raw notes and evidence from the assessment. List anything supported there │
│ that does NOT appear in the draft - omissions are as serious as errors.                            │
│   3. RECONCILE AGAINST THE CONSOLIDATED SET. The notes contain a consolidated list of findings     │
│ produced before chaining, and the chaining phase was required to carry every entry forward. Walk   │
│ that list and account for each entry: present in the draft, folded into a chain that is in the     │
│ draft, or MISSING.                                                                                 │
│   4. Treat every missing entry as a finding in its own right and recover it - it reached           │
│ consolidation on evidence, so it is supported until you disprove it. Say plainly in your output    │
│ that it was dropped between phases; that is a defect in the assessment process and the operator    │
│ needs to know the pipeline lost it, not just that the finding exists.                              │
│   5. Identify duplicates: the same underlying issue reported as several findings, or one finding   │
│ split across categories. Merge them and say so.                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Attempt to refute each claim                                                                │
│ Objective: Reproduce independently. Assume the claim is wrong.                                     │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. For every claim, reproduce it yourself against the live target rather than trusting the       │
│ quoted evidence. Evidence in a draft may be stale, partial or misread.                             │
│   2. REQUIRED for every access, bypass or authentication claim: run the CONTROL TEST - the         │
│ identical request WITHOUT the credential, token, header or material said to be responsible. If the │
│ result is unchanged, the claim is REFUTED: the resource was already reachable. Report both results │
│ side by side.                                                                                      │
│   3. For claimed code execution, require output of a chosen command returned by the target. A      │
│ payload that was sent, or an interesting error, is not execution.                                  │
│   4. Where a claim is correct but overstated, say so precisely: confirm the part that holds and    │
│ refute the part that does not.                                                                     │
│   5. Check severity against what was actually demonstrated and against breadth - one object or     │
│ all, one account or every account.                                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Return a verdict per claim                                                                  │
│ Objective: Evidence for every verdict, in both directions.                                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Assign each claim CONFIRMED, REFUTED or UNPROVEN.                                             │
│   2. CONFIRMED requires the request and response you obtained yourself, plus the control result    │
│ showing it does not occur without the material.                                                    │
│   3. REFUTED requires the evidence that disproves it - typically a control test producing the same │
│ result, or a reproduction attempt that fails.                                                      │
│   4. UNPROVEN means you could not settle it: state exactly what additional access or step would    │
│ settle it.                                                                                         │
│   5. Add any finding you confirmed that the draft omitted, with the same evidence standard.        │
│   6. Give a corrected severity for every surviving finding, and list the merges you performed.     │
│   7. WRITE EACH VERDICT AS YOU REACH IT, not once every claim is settled. Create the file when you │
│ settle your FIRST claim and append each further verdict as it is decided. This phase can stop at   │
│ any point, and a file written only at the end is the first thing lost when it does - a partial     │
│ record of real verdicts is worth far more than a complete one that was never written.              │
│   8. WRITE THE VERDICT RECORD TO DISK before you finish, at                                        │
│ `loot/assessments/<assessment>/adversarial_verdicts.md` (use the assessment directory this run is  │
│ writing to). This file is the next phase's primary input; it will not re-read the drafts you have  │
│ just been through, so anything you leave out of it is effectively dropped.                         │
│   9. Give every entry the same shape, one per claim: ID and short title; VERDICT (CONFIRMED /      │
│ REFUTED / UNPROVEN); SEVERITY; the EVIDENCE quoted verbatim - the request sent, the response       │
│ received, any value recovered - not a reference to where the evidence lives; CONTROL result; SCOPE │
│ (how many objects or accounts, and whether that was tested or inferred); REPRODUCTION steps; and   │
│ the two factor sections below.                                                                     │
│   10. COMPOUNDING FACTORS - what this finding combines with. Name the other findings that make     │
│ this one worse, and what the combination yields that neither gives alone. Material recovered here  │
│ that unlocks a surface found elsewhere belongs here, quoting the recovered value. This is where a  │
│ chain is recorded, and recording it against each part means it survives even if the phase that     │
│ built the chain is cut short. Write 'none identified' if there are none - never leave it out.      │
│   11. MITIGATING FACTORS - what this finding does NOT show. State the next step in the obvious     │
│ attack that was not attempted or not achieved, any precondition an attacker still needs, and any   │
│ control that limits it. Recovering a credential without authenticating with it, or reaching an     │
│ endpoint without extracting from it, belongs here rather than being written up as though it were   │
│ done. Write 'none identified' if there are none.                                                   │
│   12. Quote evidence in full even when it is long. The next phase can only report what this file   │
│ contains, and a pointer to a file it will not open is the same as no evidence at all.              │
│   13. REPRODUCIBILITY IS A PASS CONDITION, NOT A COURTESY. For every CONFIRMED finding, write the  │
│ exact steps that reproduce it: the full request or command, the required preconditions, and the    │
│ specific part of the response that demonstrates it. Someone with no access to this assessment must │
│ be able to follow them and get the same result. If you cannot write those steps, the finding is    │
│ UNPROVEN no matter how confident you are.                                                          │
│   14. State the SCOPE of every confirmed finding explicitly: how many objects, accounts or         │
│ endpoints it affects, and whether you established that by testing or by inference. 'One account'   │
│ and 'every account' are different findings at different severities, and an untested assumption     │
│ about breadth must be labelled as one.                                                             │
│   15. Before finishing, verify each confirmed finding carries all four: scope, validation          │
│ (including its control test), evidence (the actual request and response), and reproduction steps.  │
│ Name any finding missing one of them rather than passing it through silently - the final gate      │
│ cannot report what it cannot check.                                                                │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

